/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ai-900.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ai-900", [["aif1",0,1,"A retailer wants to estimate how many umbrellas each store will sell next week using past sales and the weather forecast. Which AI workload is this?",["Anomaly detection","Computer vision","Prediction and forecasting","Knowledge mining"],2,"Estimating a future quantity from historical data is prediction or forecasting, usually built with a regression model. Anomaly detection looks for unusual values rather than estimating future ones.","Identify features of common AI workloads",["Anomaly detection flags unusual values against a normal pattern; it does not estimate a future sales quantity.","Computer vision interprets images and video, and this scenario uses sales and weather data, not pictures.",null,"Knowledge mining makes large volumes of documents searchable; it does not forecast demand."],null],["aif2",0,1,"A bank wants to flag card transactions that look very different from a customer's normal spending so they can be reviewed. Which workload fits best?",["Anomaly detection","Speech recognition","Generative AI","Object detection"],0,"Anomaly detection learns what normal looks like and flags unusual events such as fraudulent transactions. Generative AI creates new content and is not the core technique for spotting outliers.","Identify features of common AI workloads",[null,"Speech recognition converts spoken audio into text and has nothing to do with card transaction data.","Generative AI creates new content such as text or images; spotting unusual transactions is a detection task.","Object detection locates objects in images, not unusual patterns in payment records."],null],["aif3",0,1,"A city council wants software that reads number plates from traffic camera images. Which AI workload does this use?",["Natural language processing","Computer vision","Anomaly detection","Conversational AI"],1,"Reading text from images is computer vision (specifically optical character recognition). NLP works on text that is already digital, not on pixels in an image.","Identify computer vision workloads",["NLP works on text that is already digital; reading characters from a camera image is a vision task first.",null,"Anomaly detection flags unusual events in data, but reading plate characters is text extraction from images.","Conversational AI powers bots that hold dialogs with users, not reading plates from traffic images."],null],["aif4",0,1,"A legal firm has thousands of scanned contracts and wants staff to search them by party names, dates and clauses. Which workload describes this?",["Speech synthesis","Regression","Anomaly detection","Knowledge mining"],3,"Knowledge mining extracts information from large volumes of unstructured content (often with OCR and entity extraction) and builds a searchable index. Regression predicts numbers and does not make content searchable.","Identify knowledge mining and document processing workloads",["Speech synthesis turns text into spoken audio and does not make scanned contracts searchable.","Regression predicts a numeric value; it does not extract or index information from documents.","Anomaly detection finds unusual data points, not names, dates and clauses across a document collection.",null],null],["aif5",0,1,"Which statement best describes how a machine learning model differs from traditional rule-based software?",["It runs only in the cloud, whereas rule-based software runs on devices","It always returns the correct answer because it was trained on real data","It learns the relationship between inputs and outputs from example data","It needs no data because a developer writes its decision rules by hand"],2,"A model learns patterns from training data instead of following hand-written rules. Its outputs are probabilistic, so it can be wrong, and models can run in the cloud or on devices.","Identify features of common AI workloads",["Where software runs is not the difference; models can run in the cloud or on devices, and so can rule-based code.","Models give probability-based predictions and can be wrong, even when trained on real data.",null,"This describes rule-based software, the opposite of a model that learns its logic from example data."],null],["aif6",0,1,"A support team wants a tool that drafts a polite reply to each customer email, which an agent edits before sending. Which workload is this?",["Generative AI","Image classification","Anomaly detection","Knowledge mining"],0,"Creating new text from a prompt (the customer email) is generative AI. Knowledge mining indexes existing content for search but does not write new replies.","Identify features of generative AI workloads",[null,"Image classification assigns labels to images; the task here is writing new text replies to emails.","Anomaly detection flags unusual events; it does not draft replies to customers.","Knowledge mining indexes existing content for search but does not write new, tailored replies."],null],["aif7",0,1,"A loan approval model rejects applicants from one postcode far more often than equally qualified applicants elsewhere. Which responsible AI principle is most directly at risk?",["Transparency","Inclusiveness","Reliability and safety","Fairness"],3,"Fairness means similar people should get similar outcomes, without bias tied to attributes such as location used as a proxy for ethnicity. Inclusiveness is about designing for everyone to be able to use the system, which is a different concern.","Describe considerations for fairness in an AI solution",["Transparency is about explaining how the system works; the core problem here is unequal outcomes for similar applicants.","Inclusiveness is about everyone being able to use the system, not about biased decisions between groups.","Reliability and safety concerns consistent, safe operation, not systematically different outcomes for one postcode.",null],null],["aif8",0,1,"A company designs its voice assistant so it also works with a screen reader and supports users with speech impairments. Which principle does this demonstrate?",["Accountability","Inclusiveness","Transparency","Privacy and security"],1,"Inclusiveness means empowering everyone and engaging people of all abilities. Accountability is about people being answerable for how the system operates, not accessibility.","Describe considerations for inclusiveness in an AI solution",["Accountability is about who answers for the system through governance, not about accessibility for users.",null,"Transparency concerns explaining how the system works, not making it usable by people with disabilities.","Privacy and security is about protecting data, not about designing for screen readers and speech impairments."],null],["aif9",0,1,"An autonomous warehouse robot is tested extensively under unusual conditions and has a safe fallback if its sensors fail. Which principle is being applied?",["Reliability and safety","Fairness","Inclusiveness","Transparency"],0,"Reliability and safety means the system performs as intended, including in unexpected conditions, and fails safely. Transparency concerns explaining how the system works, not how it behaves under failure.","Describe considerations for reliability and safety in an AI solution",[null,"Fairness concerns equal outcomes across groups of people, not how a robot behaves when sensors fail.","Inclusiveness is about making systems usable by everyone, not about testing edge conditions and failing safely.","Transparency concerns explaining the system's behavior, not engineering a safe fallback for failures."],null],["aif10",0,1,"Customers can see which factors most influenced an AI-driven insurance quote and read documentation on the model's limitations. Which principle does this support?",["Accountability","Reliability and safety","Transparency","Fairness"],2,"Transparency means people understand how an AI system works and what influences its outputs. Accountability is about who is responsible for the system, and fairness about equal treatment.","Describe considerations for transparency in an AI solution",["Accountability is about people being answerable for the system; showing decision factors is about understanding it.","Reliability and safety concerns consistent and safe performance, not explaining which factors drove a quote.",null,"Fairness concerns equal treatment of groups; disclosing influencing factors and limitations is transparency."],null],["aif11",0,1,"An organization sets up a review board that signs off AI systems before release and owns the response when they cause harm. Which principle is this?",["Transparency","Accountability","Inclusiveness","Privacy and security"],1,"Accountability means the people who design and deploy AI are answerable for it, through governance such as review boards and clear ownership. Transparency is about explaining behavior, not assigning responsibility.","Describe considerations for accountability in an AI solution",["Transparency is about explaining how the system works, not assigning ownership and sign-off responsibility.",null,"Inclusiveness is about designing for all users, not about governance and answering for harm.","Privacy and security is about protecting data, not about a board that owns decisions and their consequences."],null],["aif12",0,1,"A health app's model is trained on patient records. The team removes names, encrypts the data and limits who can access it. Which principle is being addressed?",["Fairness","Transparency","Inclusiveness","Privacy and security"],3,"Protecting personal data and controlling access supports the privacy and security principle. Fairness concerns biased outcomes, which anonymization alone does not guarantee.","Describe considerations for privacy and security in an AI solution",["Fairness concerns equal outcomes for groups; removing names and encrypting data protects personal information.","Transparency is about explaining the system, not about encryption and access control.","Inclusiveness is about accessibility for all users, not about protecting patient records.",null],null],["aif13",0,1,"Why does Microsoft require an application and approval before customers can use face identification in Azure AI Face?",["Face identification is still in preview and has no pricing yet","It is a Limited Access feature because misuse could cause serious harm","Face identification only works in regions that need special contracts","Approval is needed to raise the free tier's monthly transaction limit"],1,"Face identification and verification are Limited Access features: Microsoft reviews the customer and use case because of the potential for harm such as surveillance. The requirement is about responsible use, not pricing, preview status or quotas.","Identify guiding principles for responsible AI",["The requirement is not about preview status or pricing; it exists because of the potential for harmful misuse.",null,"Face identification is not restricted to special-contract regions; the approval is about responsible use.","Limited Access approval does not raise free-tier quotas; it gates sensitive capabilities by customer and use case."],null],["aif14",0,1,"A mobile app lets users photograph a plant and tells them its species. Which workload is this?",["Image classification","Speech recognition","Anomaly detection","Knowledge mining"],0,"Assigning one label (the species) to a whole image is image classification, a computer vision workload. Anomaly detection looks for unusual data points, not categories of images.","Identify computer vision workloads",[null,"Speech recognition converts audio to text, but the input here is a photo of a plant.","Anomaly detection flags unusual data points, not which species a photo shows.","Knowledge mining indexes large sets of documents for search, not labeling a single photo."],null],["aif15",0,1,"A call centre wants live captions of phone calls and a bot that answers routine questions by voice. Which workloads are involved?",["Computer vision and knowledge mining","Anomaly detection and regression","Speech and conversational AI","Document processing and forecasting"],2,"Captions need speech to text, and a voice bot needs conversational AI (understanding requests and replying, often with text to speech). None of the other pairs deal with audio or dialog.","Identify natural language processing workloads",["Computer vision and knowledge mining work with images and document search, not phone audio and dialog.","Anomaly detection and regression find outliers and predict numbers; neither transcribes calls or answers by voice.",null,"Document processing and forecasting extract form fields and predict values, not captions or voice bots."],null],["aif16",0,1,"An accounts team wants to pull the vendor name, invoice total and due date out of PDF invoices automatically. Which workload is this?",["Generative AI","Anomaly detection","Speech synthesis","Document processing"],3,"Document processing (document intelligence) extracts structured fields and values from forms and documents such as invoices. Generative AI creates new content rather than reliably extracting existing fields.","Identify document processing workloads",["Generative AI creates new content; reliably pulling existing fields from invoices is a document processing task.","Anomaly detection flags unusual values but does not extract vendor names and totals from PDFs.","Speech synthesis produces spoken audio from text and does not read invoices.",null],null],["aif17",0,1,"A team publishes a document describing their AI system's intended uses, its limitations and how it was tested. Which kind of Microsoft artifact is this modeled on?",["A service level agreement","A transparency note","A data processing addendum","A Limited Access form"],1,"Microsoft publishes transparency notes for its AI services describing capabilities, intended uses and limitations. An SLA covers uptime commitments, not how the AI behaves.","Identify guiding principles for responsible AI",["An SLA commits to uptime and service credits, not to describing an AI system's intended uses and limitations.",null,"A data processing addendum is a contractual privacy document, not a description of how an AI system behaves.","A Limited Access form is an application to use restricted features, not published documentation of limitations."],null],["aif18",0,2,"A dataset of used cars has columns for mileage, age, engine size and sale price. You want to predict sale price. What is the sale price column?",["A feature","The label","A cluster","A hyperparameter"],1,"The label is the value the model learns to predict. Mileage, age and engine size are features, the inputs the model uses to make the prediction.","Describe features and labels in a dataset",["Features are the inputs such as mileage and age; the value being predicted is the label.",null,"A cluster is a group found by unsupervised learning, not a column you are trying to predict.","Hyperparameters are training settings chosen by the data scientist, not a column in the dataset."],null],["aif19",0,2,"A utility company wants to predict the number of kilowatt-hours a household will use next month. Which type of machine learning is this?",["Regression","Binary classification","Clustering","Multiclass classification"],0,"Predicting a numeric value such as energy use is regression. Classification predicts a category, and clustering groups unlabeled items.","Identify regression machine learning scenarios",[null,"Binary classification predicts one of two categories, but energy use is a continuous number.","Clustering groups unlabeled items and does not predict a numeric value for each household.","Multiclass classification picks one of several categories, while kilowatt-hours are a numeric quantity."],null],["aif20",0,2,"A hospital wants to predict whether a patient will be readmitted within 30 days: yes or no. Which technique fits?",["Regression","Clustering","Binary classification","Multiclass classification"],2,"Two possible outcomes make this binary classification. Multiclass classification would apply if there were three or more categories, such as low, medium or high risk.","Identify classification machine learning scenarios",["Regression predicts a number, but readmitted yes or no is a category with two values.","Clustering is unsupervised grouping with no known labels, while readmission outcomes are known labels.",null,"Multiclass classification needs three or more classes; yes or no is only two."],null],["aif21",0,2,"A marketing team has purchase histories but no predefined customer categories, and wants to discover groups of customers who behave alike. Which technique should they use?",["Regression","Binary classification","Multiclass classification","Clustering"],3,"Clustering is unsupervised: it finds groups of similar items in data with no labels. Classification needs labeled examples of each known category.","Identify clustering machine learning scenarios",["Regression predicts a numeric value and needs a label, but here there are no categories or targets at all.","Binary classification needs labeled examples of two known classes, which the team does not have.","Multiclass classification needs predefined labeled categories, and the team wants to discover groups instead.",null],null],["aif22",0,2,"A support system must route each ticket to one of five teams: billing, shipping, returns, technical or account. Which technique is this?",["Multiclass classification","Regression","Clustering","Binary classification"],0,"Choosing one label from more than two known classes is multiclass classification. Clustering would be used if the categories were not known in advance.","Identify classification machine learning scenarios",[null,"Regression predicts a number, not which of five named teams should receive a ticket.","Clustering discovers unknown groups; here the five teams are known categories to predict.","Binary classification has only two classes, while this task has five."],null],["aif23",0,2,"A model scores 99% accuracy on its training data but only 62% on validation data. What is the most likely explanation?",["The model is underfitting because it is too simple","The validation set was used to train the model too","The model is overfitting and has memorized the training data","The label column was accidentally removed from the data"],2,"A large gap between training and validation performance is the classic sign of overfitting. If validation data had been used for training, the scores would look similar rather than diverge, and underfitting shows poor scores on both.","Describe core machine learning concepts",["Underfitting gives poor scores on both training and validation data, not 99% on training.","If validation data had been used for training, validation scores would look high too, not far lower.",null,"A missing label column would stop supervised training altogether rather than produce this score gap."],null],["aif24",0,2,"Why do data scientists split their data into training and validation sets?",["To reduce the cost of storing the data in Azure","To evaluate the model on data it did not see during training","To make training faster by using only half of the features","To create labels for data that was originally unlabeled"],1,"Holding back validation data shows how well the model generalizes to new data. The split does not create labels or remove features.","Describe core machine learning concepts",["Splitting data does not reduce storage cost; all of the data is still stored.",null,"The split is by rows, not by features; every feature is still used in training.","Splitting data cannot create labels; supervised training needs labels before the split."],null],["aif25",0,2,"You evaluate a regression model that predicts house prices. Which metric tells you the proportion of the variance in prices that the model explains, where closer to 1 is better?",["Precision","Recall","Mean absolute error","Coefficient of determination (R²)"],3,"R² measures how much of the variance in the label the model explains, with 1 being a perfect fit. MAE is an average error in the label's units, and precision and recall are classification metrics.","Describe regression evaluation metrics",["Precision is a classification metric about predicted positives and does not apply to price predictions.","Recall is a classification metric about found positives, not variance explained in a numeric label.","MAE is the average error in the label's units; it does not express a proportion of variance explained.",null],null],["aif26",0,2,"A fraud model must catch as many real fraud cases as possible, even if some legitimate transactions are flagged for review. Which metric should be prioritized?",["Recall","Precision","R²","Root mean squared error"],0,"Recall measures the share of actual positives (real fraud) that the model finds. Precision measures how many flagged cases were really fraud, which matters more when false alarms are costly.","Describe classification evaluation metrics",[null,"Precision matters when false alarms are costly; here missing real fraud is the bigger concern.","R² is a regression metric and does not measure how many fraud cases a classifier catches.","RMSE is a regression error metric, not a measure of how many positives a classifier finds."],null],["aif27",0,2,"In a binary classification confusion matrix, what is a false positive?",["A negative case the model correctly predicted as negative","A positive case the model wrongly predicted as negative","A positive case the model correctly predicted as positive","A negative case the model wrongly predicted as positive"],3,"A false positive is a prediction of positive for a case that is actually negative, such as flagging a legitimate email as spam. A positive case predicted as negative is a false negative.","Describe classification evaluation metrics",["A negative case correctly predicted as negative is a true negative.","A positive case wrongly predicted as negative is a false negative, the opposite mistake.","A positive case correctly predicted as positive is a true positive.",null],null],["aif28",0,2,"Which kind of model is best suited to learning complex patterns in images, audio and natural language?",["A linear regression model","A deep neural network","A k-means clustering model","A decision table of hand-written rules"],1,"Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers that learn features directly from raw data such as pixels and audio, which is why modern vision, speech and language models use it. Linear regression cannot capture those complex patterns.","Describe deep learning concepts",["Linear regression fits simple straight-line relationships and cannot learn complex patterns in pixels or audio.",null,"K-means only groups similar items; it does not learn the layered features needed for images and language.","Hand-written rule tables cannot cover the endless variation in images, speech and natural language."],null],["aif29",0,2,"In the transformer architecture used by modern language models, what does the attention mechanism do?",["It converts audio into text before the model processes it","It removes stop words so that the input is shorter","It weighs how relevant each token is to the others in the sequence","It checks model output for harmful content before returning it"],2,"Attention lets the model consider the relationships between tokens, so the meaning of a word depends on its context. Content checks are done by separate safety systems, not by attention.","Describe features of the transformer architecture",["Converting audio to text is speech recognition, a separate step, not what attention does inside a transformer.","Attention does not delete words; it weighs relationships among all the tokens in the input.",null,"Checking output for harmful content is done by a separate safety system such as content filters."],null],["aif30",0,2,"A data scientist needs a single managed virtual machine to run Jupyter notebooks while exploring data in Azure Machine Learning. What should they create?",["A compute instance","A compute cluster","An online endpoint","A data asset"],0,"A compute instance is a managed development VM for notebooks. A compute cluster scales out for training jobs, and an online endpoint serves a deployed model.","Describe capabilities of Azure Machine Learning",[null,"A compute cluster scales out for training jobs; it is not a single personal VM for notebooks.","An online endpoint serves predictions from a deployed model, not an interactive notebook environment.","A data asset is a reference to data, not compute that can run notebooks."],null],["aif31",0,2,"An analyst with no coding experience wants Azure Machine Learning to try many algorithms on a labeled dataset and pick the best classification model. What should they use?",["The designer with a custom Python script","A compute instance running a notebook","Automated machine learning","A batch endpoint with a registered model"],2,"Automated ML tries multiple algorithms and settings and ranks the models by a chosen metric, with no code needed. Notebooks and custom scripts require coding, and an endpoint only serves a model you already have.","Describe capabilities of automated machine learning",["The designer requires choosing the algorithm yourself, and a custom Python script means writing code.","Running a notebook on a compute instance requires writing training code.",null,"A batch endpoint scores data with a model you already have; it does not train and compare algorithms."],null],["aif32",0,2,"A team wants to build a training pipeline visually by dragging data, transformation and training modules onto a canvas. Which Azure Machine Learning feature fits?",["Automated machine learning","The designer","Data labeling","The model catalog"],1,"The designer is a drag-and-drop canvas for building pipelines. Automated ML chooses algorithms for you but does not give you a module-by-module canvas.","Describe capabilities of Azure Machine Learning",["Automated ML picks and compares algorithms for you rather than giving a canvas of modules to connect.",null,"Data labeling is for tagging data with labels, not for building training pipelines.","The model catalog is for discovering and deploying existing models, not building a visual training pipeline."],null],["aif33",0,2,"A trained model must return a prediction within a second whenever a web app sends it one customer's data. How should it be deployed?",["As a batch endpoint that scores files on a schedule","As a data asset registered in the workspace","As a compute cluster that scales to zero nodes","As a real-time (online) endpoint"],3,"Online endpoints serve low-latency predictions for individual requests. Batch endpoints score large volumes of data asynchronously, and a compute cluster alone does not expose a prediction API.","Describe model deployment in Azure Machine Learning",["Batch endpoints process large volumes asynchronously, which is too slow for a one-second response per request.","Registering a data asset records data; it does not make a model available for predictions.","A compute cluster runs training or batch jobs and does not by itself expose a prediction endpoint.",null],null],["aif34",0,2,"After training a model in Azure Machine Learning, a team wants to see whether its error rate is higher for some groups of people than others. Which tool helps?",["The Responsible AI dashboard","Automated ML featurization","A compute cluster's autoscale settings","The workspace's Key Vault"],0,"The Responsible AI dashboard brings together error analysis, fairness assessment, explanations and more to find where a model performs worse. Featurization prepares data but does not assess fairness.","Describe responsible AI capabilities in Azure Machine Learning",[null,"Featurization prepares data for AutoML and does not assess how errors differ between groups.","Autoscale settings control the number of nodes, not whether the model treats groups differently.","Key Vault stores secrets and keys; it has no model fairness or error analysis features."],null],["aif35",0,3,"A quality control system must find every scratch on a product photo and return the location of each one. Which computer vision technique is required?",["Image classification","Object detection","Optical character recognition","Image captioning"],1,"Object detection returns a label and bounding box for each object found, so it can locate multiple scratches. Image classification gives a single label for the whole image with no locations.","Identify common types of computer vision solution",["Image classification gives one label for the whole image and cannot locate each scratch.",null,"OCR extracts text from images; scratches are visual defects, not characters.","Image captioning writes a sentence about the whole image and does not return the location of each scratch."],null],["aif36",0,3,"A medical imaging tool must mark exactly which pixels in a scan belong to a tumour, not just draw a box around it. Which technique is this?",["Object detection","Image classification","Semantic segmentation","Facial analysis"],2,"Semantic segmentation classifies each pixel, giving the precise shape of a region. Object detection only draws a rectangular bounding box around each object.","Identify common types of computer vision solution",["Object detection draws a rectangular box around the tumour rather than marking exactly which pixels belong to it.","Image classification labels the whole scan and gives no location or shape information.",null,"Facial analysis works on faces, not medical scans."],null],["aif37",0,3,"A photo-sharing app wants to generate a one-sentence description of each uploaded photo for users of screen readers. Which Azure AI Vision capability fits?",["Image captions","Smart crops","OCR (Read)","Face detection"],0,"Image analysis captions produce a human-readable sentence describing the image, which is ideal for alt text. Smart crops only choose a thumbnail region, and OCR only extracts text.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Vision service",[null,"Smart crops only choose a thumbnail region; they do not produce a description.","OCR extracts text that appears in the image rather than describing the scene.","Face detection locates faces but does not describe the whole photo in a sentence."],null],["aif38",0,3,"An insurer wants to extract handwritten and printed text from photos of damage claim forms, line by line. Which feature should it use?",["Image tagging","Smart crops","Face detection","OCR with the Read feature"],3,"The Read (OCR) feature extracts printed and handwritten text as lines and words with their positions. Tagging describes visual content but does not return the text on the page.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Vision service",["Image tagging returns keywords describing the scene, not the written text on the form.","Smart crops suggest a thumbnail region and do not extract any text.","Face detection finds faces and has nothing to do with reading form text.",null],null],["aif39",0,3,"What does a convolutional neural network (CNN) use to identify visual features such as edges and shapes in an image?",["Grammar rules applied to pixel names","Filters that are learned during training","Tokens that are looked up in a dictionary","A fixed list of colors defined by the developer"],1,"CNNs apply filters (kernels) across the image; the filter values are learned in training so the network picks out edges, textures and shapes. They are not hand-written rules or color lists.","Identify common types of computer vision solution",["CNNs do not use grammar rules or pixel names; they apply numeric filters to pixel values.",null,"Token dictionaries relate to language models, not how CNNs detect edges and shapes.","CNN features are learned, not a fixed color list chosen by a developer."],null],["aif40",0,3,"A retailer wants to count how many people are in each frame from a store camera, without identifying anyone. Which capability fits best?",["Face identification","OCR with the Read feature","People detection in image analysis","Custom text classification"],2,"People detection finds people in an image and returns their bounding boxes, which is enough to count them without recognizing anyone. Face identification recognizes specific individuals and needs Limited Access approval.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Vision service",["Face identification recognizes specific people, needs Limited Access and goes beyond simply counting.","OCR extracts text, and the goal is to count people, not read signs.",null,"Custom text classification labels documents, not people in camera images."],null],["aif41",0,3,"A developer wants to detect faces in photos and return whether each face is blurred or wearing glasses. Which service should they use?",["Azure AI Document Intelligence","Azure AI Language","Azure AI Translator","Azure AI Face"],3,"Azure AI Face detects faces and returns attributes such as blur, glasses, head pose and occlusion. Document Intelligence extracts data from documents and has no face attributes.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Face detection service",["Document Intelligence extracts data from documents and does not return face attributes like blur or glasses.","Azure AI Language analyzes text, not images of faces.","Azure AI Translator translates text between languages and has no face analysis.",null],null],["aif42",0,3,"A company wants employees to unlock a door by matching a live camera image to their stored photo using Azure AI Face. What must the company do first?",["Apply for and receive approval under the Limited Access policy","Switch the Face resource from the F0 tier to the S0 tier","Train a custom image classification model for each employee","Enable the emotion recognition attribute on the resource"],0,"Face verification and identification are Limited Access features that require Microsoft's approval of the customer and use case. Emotion recognition was retired, and a pricing tier change does not unlock restricted features.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Face detection service",[null,"Moving from F0 to S0 changes pricing and limits but does not unlock Limited Access features.","Face verification uses the Face service's recognition features; training an image classifier per employee is not the approach.","Emotion recognition was retired from Azure AI Face and is unrelated to matching a person to their photo."],null],["aif43",0,3,"A company processes thousands of supplier invoices and needs the invoice number, line items and totals as structured data. Which service fits best with the least effort?",["Azure AI Vision image tagging","Azure AI Document Intelligence prebuilt invoice model","Azure AI Language key phrase extraction","Azure AI Face detection"],1,"The prebuilt invoice model in Document Intelligence returns invoice fields and line items as structured data without training. Key phrase extraction works on plain text and does not understand invoice fields.","Describe capabilities of Azure AI Document Intelligence",["Image tagging describes what an image shows and does not return invoice numbers or line items.",null,"Key phrase extraction lists main topics in plain text; it does not understand invoice fields or tables.","Face detection finds faces in images and has no use for invoices."],null],["aif44",0,3,"A company's internal onboarding form has a unique layout that no prebuilt model covers. How can Document Intelligence extract its fields?",["Use the prebuilt receipt model and ignore missing fields","Convert each form to audio and use speech to text","Train a custom model using labeled sample forms","Run image captioning and parse the caption text"],2,"Custom models learn to extract fields from your own document types from a small set of labeled samples. The receipt model only understands receipts, and captions describe images rather than extract form fields.","Describe capabilities of Azure AI Document Intelligence",["The receipt model only understands receipts and would miss the onboarding form's own fields.","Converting forms to audio makes no sense; speech to text transcribes speech, not document layouts.",null,"Captions describe an image in a sentence and do not extract named form fields reliably."],null],["aif45",0,3,"Which Document Intelligence model extracts text, tables, selection marks and document structure from any document without looking for specific named fields?",["The layout model","The prebuilt ID document model","The prebuilt invoice model","A custom classification model"],0,"The layout model returns text, tables, selection marks and structure for any document. The invoice and ID models look for specific fields in those document types.","Describe capabilities of Azure AI Document Intelligence",[null,"The ID document model looks for specific identity fields such as name and date of birth.","The invoice model looks for invoice-specific fields such as vendor and total.","A custom classification model identifies the document type; it does not extract text and tables."],null],["aif46",0,3,"A developer wants one endpoint and one key to call Vision, Language and Translator from an app, with a single bill. What should they create?",["A separate single-service resource for each service","An Azure Machine Learning workspace","A compute cluster with three nodes","A multi-service Azure AI services resource"],3,"A multi-service Azure AI services resource provides one endpoint and key for several AI services with consolidated billing. Single-service resources each have their own endpoint and key.","Identify Azure tools and services for computer vision tasks",["Separate single-service resources each have their own endpoint and keys, which is the opposite of the requirement.","An Azure Machine Learning workspace is for training your own models, not calling Vision, Language and Translator.","A compute cluster runs training jobs; it provides no AI service endpoints or keys.",null],null],["aif47",0,3,"A student wants to try Azure AI Vision for a class project at no cost and accepts low transaction limits. What should they choose?",["A multi-service resource on a paid tier","A single-service Vision resource on the free F0 tier","A compute instance with a GPU","A Premium App Service plan"],1,"Single-service resources such as Azure AI Vision offer a free F0 tier with limited transactions where available. A paid multi-service resource bills for every call, and GPU compute is expensive.","Identify Azure tools and services for computer vision tasks",["A paid multi-service tier bills for every call, and the multi-service resource has no free tier.",null,"A GPU compute instance is expensive and is not needed to call a prebuilt vision API.","An App Service plan hosts web apps and does not provide Azure AI Vision capabilities."],null],["aif48",0,3,"When an app calls Azure AI Vision with a key, which two values from the resource does it need?",["The subscription ID and the tenant ID","The resource group name and the region","The endpoint URL and a resource key","The storage account name and a SAS token"],2,"Clients call the resource's endpoint URL and authenticate with one of its two keys (or with Microsoft Entra ID). Subscription and resource group names are not used to call the API.","Identify Azure tools and services for computer vision tasks",["Subscription and tenant IDs identify your Azure account, but the API call itself needs the endpoint and a key.","The resource group and region are management details, not what an app sends to call the API.",null,"Storage account names and SAS tokens are for Azure Storage, not for authenticating to Azure AI Vision."],null],["aif49",0,3,"A news site wants to automatically choose the most interesting region of each photo for square thumbnails. Which image analysis feature helps?",["Smart crops","Dense captions","OCR (Read)","Background removal of text"],0,"Smart crops suggest a crop region around the area of interest for a given aspect ratio. Dense captions describe regions in words but do not produce thumbnail crops.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Vision service",[null,"Dense captions describe regions of an image in words but do not produce thumbnail crops.","OCR extracts text from the image rather than choosing an interesting region.","This is not a real image analysis feature, and removing text does not choose a thumbnail region."],null],["aif50",0,3,"A security team asks for a system that infers the emotional state of shoppers from camera images using Azure AI Face. What should you tell them?",["Emotion inference requires only the free F0 tier","Emotion inference must be enabled with a custom model","Emotion inference is available only in Document Intelligence","Azure AI Face no longer offers emotion recognition"],3,"Microsoft retired emotion, gender and age inference from Azure AI Face as part of its responsible AI commitments. No tier or custom model restores it, and Document Intelligence does not analyze faces.","Describe capabilities of the Azure AI Face detection service",["No pricing tier, free or paid, restores emotion inference, because the capability was retired.","Emotion inference cannot be re-enabled with a custom model in Azure AI Face; it was withdrawn.","Document Intelligence extracts data from documents and has never analyzed faces or emotions.",null],null],["aif51",0,3,"A newer type of vision model can answer a free-text question about an image, such as 'Is the fire exit blocked?'. What kind of model is this?",["A regression model trained on pixel values","A multimodal model that combines images and language","A clustering model that groups similar photos","A rule-based OCR engine"],1,"Multimodal models are trained on both images and text, so they can describe images and answer questions about them. OCR only extracts text, and clustering only groups images.","Identify common types of computer vision solution",["A regression model predicts a number and cannot answer a free-text question about an image.",null,"A clustering model groups similar images but cannot understand and answer a question.","Rule-based OCR only extracts text and cannot reason about whether an exit is blocked."],null],["aif52",0,4,"A hotel chain wants to know whether each online review is positive, negative or neutral. Which Azure AI Language feature should it use?",["Key phrase extraction","Sentiment analysis","Entity linking","Language detection"],1,"Sentiment analysis returns positive, neutral or negative labels with confidence scores. Key phrase extraction lists the main talking points but does not judge their tone.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Key phrase extraction lists talking points but does not say whether the tone is positive or negative.",null,"Entity linking maps mentions to knowledge base entries and does not judge sentiment.","Language detection identifies which language the review is in, not how the writer feels."],null],["aif53",0,4,"A review says 'The room was spotless but the breakfast was cold.' The hotel wants the sentiment toward 'room' and 'breakfast' separately. What should it enable?",["Language detection","Named entity recognition","Opinion mining","Custom text classification"],2,"Opinion mining (aspect-based sentiment) links opinions to specific targets such as room and breakfast. Document-level sentiment would give only one overall label for mixed text like this.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Language detection only identifies the language, not opinions about the room or breakfast.","NER finds and categorizes entities such as people and places but does not attach sentiment to them.",null,"Custom text classification assigns your own labels to whole documents, not per-aspect sentiment."],null],["aif54",0,4,"A global support inbox receives messages in many languages and must route each one to a team that speaks that language. Which feature identifies the language?",["Language detection","Speech synthesis","Key phrase extraction","Question answering"],0,"Language detection returns the language of the text with a confidence score. Translator can also detect language, but among these options, language detection is the feature designed for this.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",[null,"Speech synthesis produces audio from text and does not identify a message's language.","Key phrase extraction returns main topics, not the language of the text.","Question answering returns answers from a knowledge base and does not route by language."],null],["aif55",0,4,"A news aggregator wants to list the main talking points of each article, such as 'interest rates' or 'housing market'. Which feature fits?",["Sentiment analysis","PII detection","Speech to text","Key phrase extraction"],3,"Key phrase extraction returns the main concepts in a text. Sentiment analysis reports tone, and PII detection finds personal data.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Sentiment analysis reports tone, not the topics an article talks about.","PII detection finds personal data such as phone numbers, not the article's main points.","Speech to text transcribes audio, and these articles are already written text.",null],null],["aif56",0,4,"A law firm needs to find people, organizations, dates and locations mentioned in contracts. Which feature should it use?",["Named entity recognition","Language detection","Opinion mining","Speech translation"],0,"Named entity recognition identifies and categorizes entities such as people, organizations, dates and locations. Opinion mining finds opinions about aspects, not entities.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",[null,"Language detection only identifies the language of the contract, not the entities mentioned in it.","Opinion mining links opinions to aspects; it does not list people, organizations and dates.","Speech translation works on spoken audio, not on extracting entities from written contracts."],null],["aif57",0,4,"Before sharing chat transcripts with a vendor, a company must find and mask phone numbers, email addresses and card numbers. Which feature fits?",["Entity linking","Key phrase extraction","PII detection","Sentiment analysis"],2,"PII detection identifies personal information and can return redacted text. Entity linking connects entities to a knowledge base and does not redact anything.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Entity linking connects mentions to knowledge base entries and does not find or mask phone and card numbers.","Key phrase extraction lists main topics and does not detect or redact personal data.",null,"Sentiment analysis judges tone and does nothing to hide personal information."],null],["aif58",0,4,"A text mentions 'Mercury'. The app must know whether this refers to the planet, the element or the musician, and link to the right reference page. Which feature does this?",["Language detection","Entity linking","Summarization","Custom text classification"],1,"Entity linking disambiguates an entity using context and links it to a knowledge base entry. Summarization shortens text but does not resolve which Mercury is meant.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",["Language detection says which language the text is in, not which Mercury is meant.",null,"Summarization shortens text but does not resolve an ambiguous entity to a reference page.","Custom text classification labels whole documents with your categories and does not disambiguate a single entity."],null],["aif59",0,4,"A semantic search system finds articles similar in meaning to a query even when they share no words. What does it compare?",["The number of characters in each text","Embedding vectors that represent the meaning of the text","The language code returned by language detection","The order in which words appear in each text"],1,"Embeddings are vectors in which texts with similar meaning sit close together, so similarity can be measured even without shared words. Character counts and word order do not capture meaning.","Describe features of language models",["Character counts say nothing about meaning, so they cannot match texts that use different words.",null,"The language code only tells you which language a text is in, not what it means.","Word order alone cannot match texts that share no words; meaning is captured by embeddings."],null],["aif60",0,4,"A legal team wants a short paragraph that captures the key points of each long court ruling. Which Azure AI Language feature fits?",["Summarization","Key phrase extraction","Language detection","Named entity recognition"],0,"Summarization produces a condensed version of the text, either by selecting key sentences (extractive) or by writing new ones (abstractive). Key phrase extraction returns separate phrases, not a readable paragraph.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",[null,"Key phrase extraction returns a list of separate phrases, not a readable summary paragraph.","Language detection identifies the language and does not condense the ruling.","NER lists entities such as parties and dates, not the key points of the ruling."],null],["aif61",0,4,"A company wants meeting recordings turned into written transcripts. Which capability of Azure AI Speech should it use?",["Text to speech","Speaker voice creation","Language detection of text","Speech to text"],3,"Speech to text (speech recognition) converts spoken audio into text. Text to speech does the opposite, producing audio from text.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Text to speech goes the other way, producing audio from text rather than transcripts from audio.","Creating a custom synthetic voice produces speech output; it does not transcribe recordings.","Language detection works on text that already exists, not on meeting audio.",null],null],["aif62",0,4,"A navigation app must read directions aloud to drivers in a natural-sounding voice. Which capability should it use?",["Speech to text","Text to speech","Speech translation","Conversational language understanding"],1,"Text to speech (speech synthesis) turns text into spoken audio using neural voices. Speech to text converts audio into text, which is the opposite direction.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Speech to text converts audio into text, the opposite of reading directions aloud.",null,"Speech translation converts speech into another language, which the app does not need.","CLU predicts intents and entities from user input; it does not produce spoken audio."],null],["aif63",0,4,"An e-commerce site must translate product descriptions from English into French, German and Japanese. Which service is designed for this?",["Azure AI Language","Azure AI Speech text to speech","Azure AI Translator","Azure AI Document Intelligence"],2,"Azure AI Translator translates text (and whole documents) between many languages, including into several targets in one request. Azure AI Language analyzes text but does not translate it.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Azure AI Language analyzes text for sentiment, entities and more, but it does not translate.","Text to speech produces audio from text in the same language; it does not translate descriptions.",null,"Document Intelligence extracts fields from documents and does not translate them."],null],["aif64",0,4,"At a conference, a speaker's English speech must appear as live Spanish subtitles. Which capability fits best?",["Speech translation in Azure AI Speech","Document translation in Azure AI Translator","Sentiment analysis in Azure AI Language","Key phrase extraction in Azure AI Language"],0,"Speech translation takes spoken audio and returns translated text (or speech) in real time. Document translation works on files, not live audio.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",[null,"Document translation works on stored files asynchronously, not on live spoken audio.","Sentiment analysis judges tone and does not translate the speech into Spanish.","Key phrase extraction lists main topics and neither transcribes nor translates speech."],null],["aif65",0,4,"A travel app user says 'Book me a flight to Lisbon next Friday'. The app must work out that the user wants to book a flight and capture Lisbon and next Friday. Which service does this?",["Question answering","Conversational language understanding","Azure AI Translator","Key phrase extraction"],1,"Conversational language understanding predicts the intent (BookFlight) and extracts entities (destination, date) from an utterance. Question answering matches questions to stored answers but does not extract entities for an action.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Question answering returns stored answers from a knowledge base; it does not extract a destination and date for an action.",null,"Translator changes the language of text but does not work out what the user wants to do.","Key phrase extraction lists topics but does not identify the user's intent or label entities for booking."],null],["aif66",0,4,"In a conversational language understanding project, what is 'What's the weather in Oslo tomorrow?' an example of?",["An intent","An entity","A label for a regression model","An utterance"],3,"An utterance is an example of something a user might say. The intent is the goal behind it (GetWeather), and Oslo and tomorrow are entities.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["The intent is the goal behind the sentence, such as GetWeather, not the example sentence itself.","An entity is a detail inside the sentence, such as Oslo or tomorrow, not the whole sentence.","CLU does not use regression labels; the example sentence is training input, not a numeric target.",null],null],["aif67",0,4,"A council has a web page of 200 frequently asked questions and wants a bot that answers residents from those pairs. Which feature should it use?",["Custom text classification","Speech synthesis","Question answering","Entity linking"],2,"Question answering builds a knowledge base from existing FAQ content and returns the best matching answer. Text classification assigns labels but does not return answers.","Identify Azure tools and services for NLP workloads",["Custom text classification assigns categories to text but does not return answers to questions.","Speech synthesis turns text into audio; it does not find the right answer to a question.",null,"Entity linking maps mentions to knowledge base entries and does not answer residents' questions."],null],["aif68",0,4,"A company wants incoming emails sorted into its own categories: complaint, refund, order change and feedback. No prebuilt model knows these categories. What should it use?",["Custom text classification trained on labeled emails","Prebuilt sentiment analysis","Language detection","Prebuilt named entity recognition"],0,"Custom text classification learns your own categories from labeled examples. Sentiment analysis only reports positive, neutral or negative tone.","Identify features of common NLP workload scenarios",[null,"Prebuilt sentiment analysis only returns positive, neutral or negative, not the company's own categories.","Language detection only identifies the language of each email.","Prebuilt NER finds entities such as people and dates and cannot sort emails into custom categories."],null],["aif69",0,5,"How does a large language model produce a response to a prompt?",["It looks up the answer in a database of stored responses","It copies the closest matching web page it saw in training","It runs hand-written grammar rules for each supported language","It predicts one likely next token at a time based on the text so far"],3,"LLMs generate text by repeatedly predicting a probable next token and appending it. They do not retrieve stored answers or copy pages, which is also why they can produce fluent but wrong statements.","Identify features of generative AI models",["LLMs do not look answers up in a response database; they generate text token by token.","Models do not copy stored web pages; they generate new text from learned probabilities.","LLMs learn patterns from data rather than running hand-written grammar rules per language.",null],null],["aif70",0,5,"A developer notices that the text 'unbelievable' is counted as more than one unit when calculating the size of a prompt. What are these units called?",["Embeddings","Parameters","Tokens","Epochs"],2,"Models process text as tokens, which can be whole words, parts of words or punctuation; limits and billing are counted in tokens. Embeddings are vectors that represent meaning, not the units of text.","Identify features of generative AI models",["Embeddings are vectors that represent meaning, not the units of text counted in a prompt.","Parameters are a model's learned weights, not pieces of the prompt text.",null,"Epochs are complete passes through training data, not units of text."],null],["aif71",0,5,"A sales team uses an assistant built into their CRM that drafts follow-up emails and summarizes account history on request. What is this kind of generative AI app commonly called?",["A knowledge mining index","A regression model","A clustering pipeline","A copilot"],3,"A copilot is a generative AI assistant embedded in an app that helps users with tasks such as drafting and summarizing, while the user stays in control. A regression model only predicts numbers.","Identify common scenarios for generative AI",["A knowledge mining index makes content searchable; it does not draft emails inside a CRM.","A regression model predicts a number and cannot draft or summarize text.","A clustering pipeline groups similar records and does not assist users with writing tasks.",null],null],["aif72",0,5,"A chat app must always answer as a formal assistant for Contoso, refuse off-topic requests and reply in bullet points. Where should these instructions go?",["In the system message","In the temperature setting","In the model's training data","In the content filter configuration"],0,"The system message sets the model's role, rules and output format for the whole conversation. Temperature controls randomness, and retraining the model is not needed for instructions like these.","Identify prompt engineering techniques",[null,"Temperature only controls randomness; it cannot hold role, refusal rules or format instructions.","Retraining the model is unnecessary and expensive for instructions a system message handles.","Content filters block harmful content categories; they do not set persona or bullet-point formatting."],null],["aif73",0,5,"You include two example questions with the exact answer format you want before the user's real question. What is this technique called?",["Grounding with RAG","Zero-shot prompting","Few-shot prompting","Fine-tuning"],2,"Providing a few worked examples in the prompt is few-shot prompting. Zero-shot gives no examples, and fine-tuning changes the model with additional training rather than examples in the prompt.","Identify prompt engineering techniques",["Grounding with RAG adds retrieved source documents, not worked examples of the answer format.","Zero-shot prompting means giving no examples, which is the opposite of this technique.",null,"Fine-tuning changes the model with additional training rather than putting examples in the prompt."],null],["aif74",0,5,"A company's HR chatbot must answer questions using the current employee handbook, which the model never saw in training, without retraining the model. Which approach fits?",["Raise the temperature so the model is more creative","Use a larger model with more parameters","Train a new model from scratch on the handbook","Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) over the handbook"],3,"RAG retrieves relevant handbook passages at question time and adds them to the prompt so the model answers from them. A larger model still has not seen the handbook, and higher temperature increases randomness.","Identify features of generative AI solutions",["Raising temperature adds randomness and makes invented answers more likely, not less.","A larger model still has never seen the current handbook, so it cannot answer from it.","Training a new model from scratch is hugely expensive and would be stale as soon as the handbook changes.",null],null],["aif75",0,5,"In a RAG solution, what usually happens first when a user asks a question?",["The model generates an answer and then searches for sources","Relevant passages are retrieved from an index of your data","The model is fine-tuned on the user's question","The content filter rewrites the question in simpler words"],1,"RAG retrieves relevant content first (often by vector or hybrid search), adds it to the prompt, then the model generates a grounded answer. Fine-tuning is a separate training process and is not done per question.","Identify features of generative AI solutions",["RAG retrieves before generating; generating first would leave the answer ungrounded.",null,"Fine-tuning is a separate training process and is not run for each user question.","Content filters check for harmful content; they do not rewrite questions as a RAG step."],null],["aif76",0,5,"An app extracts dates and totals from text with an LLM and needs consistent, repeatable output. How should temperature be set?",["High, near the maximum, for varied output","It does not matter because temperature only affects images","Low, near 0, for focused and deterministic output","Temperature should be removed from the request entirely"],2,"Low temperature makes the model pick the most likely tokens, giving consistent answers suited to extraction and facts. High temperature increases randomness, which is better for brainstorming and creative writing.","Identify features of generative AI models",["High temperature increases randomness, which works against consistent, repeatable extraction.","Temperature applies to text generation in language models, not only to images.",null,"Removing it just uses the default value, which is not guaranteed to give deterministic output."],null],["aif77",0,5,"A marketing team wants a model to generate brand-new product images from text descriptions. Which kind of model should they deploy?",["An embeddings model","A speech to text model","A named entity recognition model","An image generation model"],3,"Image generation models create images from text prompts. Embeddings models turn text into vectors for search and similarity and cannot produce images.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["An embeddings model outputs vectors for search and similarity, not images.","Speech to text transcribes audio and cannot create product images.","NER finds entities in text and has no image generation capability.",null],null],["aif78",0,5,"To build semantic search over product manuals for a RAG app, you need to convert each passage into a vector. Which model type does this?",["An embeddings model","A GPT chat completion model","An image generation model","A text to speech model"],0,"Embeddings models return vectors that represent meaning, which are stored in a vector index for similarity search. A chat model generates text rather than vectors.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",[null,"A chat completion model generates text responses, not the vectors a semantic index needs.","An image generation model creates pictures, not vectors for text passages.","Text to speech produces audio from text, not vectors for search."],null],["aif79",0,5,"Where in Microsoft Foundry can you compare available models from Microsoft, OpenAI and other providers, read their model cards and choose one to deploy?",["The Responsible AI dashboard","The Azure Machine Learning designer","Document Intelligence Studio","The model catalog"],3,"The model catalog in Microsoft Foundry lists models from many providers with model cards and deployment options. The designer builds ML pipelines, and Document Intelligence Studio is for document models.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["The Responsible AI dashboard assesses trained ML models for errors and fairness; it does not list provider models.","The designer builds ML training pipelines and does not list models from other providers.","Document Intelligence Studio is for document extraction models, not generative models from many providers.",null],null],["aif80",0,5,"A developer wants to try prompts and a system message against a deployed chat model in a browser before writing any code. What should they use?",["An Azure Machine Learning compute cluster","Language Studio's sentiment analysis page","The chat playground in the Foundry portal","The Azure pricing calculator"],2,"The chat playground lets you test a deployed model with system messages, parameters and your own data without code. A compute cluster runs training jobs, not interactive prompt testing.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["A compute cluster runs training jobs and offers no interactive prompt testing.","Sentiment analysis in Language Studio analyzes text tone and cannot test a chat model's system message.",null,"The pricing calculator estimates costs; it does not run prompts against a model."],null],["aif81",0,5,"Which statement about Azure OpenAI models in Microsoft Foundry is correct?",["The models run only on devices and never in Azure","They are Azure-hosted OpenAI models with Azure security and content filtering","They are free to use with no token-based billing","They require you to train each model from scratch on your own data"],1,"Azure OpenAI provides OpenAI models such as GPT chat and embeddings models hosted in Azure, with Azure networking, identity and built-in content filtering. Usage is billed, and the models are pretrained.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["Azure OpenAI models are hosted in Azure and called over an endpoint, not restricted to devices.",null,"Azure OpenAI usage is billed, typically per token; there is no free tier.","The models are pretrained; you use them through prompts and grounding, not by training from scratch."],null],["aif82",0,5,"A help desk solution uses a model that can look up order status by calling an internal API, search the knowledge base and open a ticket to complete a user's request. What is this solution best described as?",["A clustering model","An OCR pipeline","A regression endpoint","An AI agent"],3,"An agent combines a model with instructions, knowledge and tools such as API calls so it can take actions toward a goal. The other options predict numbers, read text or group data and cannot take actions.","Identify features of generative AI solutions",["A clustering model only groups similar data and cannot call APIs or open tickets.","An OCR pipeline extracts text from images and cannot take actions for a user.","A regression endpoint returns numeric predictions and cannot look up orders or complete tasks.",null],null],["aif83",0,5,"Which Microsoft Foundry capability is designed for building, deploying and managing agents that use models together with tools and knowledge sources?",["Azure AI Document Intelligence","Azure AI Translator","Foundry Agent Service","Azure AI Face"],2,"Foundry Agent Service (formerly Azure AI Agent Service) hosts agents that combine a model, instructions, tools and knowledge. The other services are single-purpose AI services.","Identify generative AI services and capabilities in Microsoft Azure",["Document Intelligence extracts data from documents; it is not a platform for building agents.","Translator translates text and documents; it does not host agents with tools.",null,"Azure AI Face detects and analyzes faces and has nothing to do with building agents."],null],["aif84",0,5,"Microsoft's guidance for responsible generative AI describes four stages. Which order is correct?",["Identify, measure, mitigate, operate","Measure, identify, operate, mitigate","Mitigate, identify, measure, operate","Operate, mitigate, measure, identify"],0,"You first identify potential harms, then measure how often and how severely they occur, then mitigate them in layers, and finally operate the solution responsibly with monitoring and incident response.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",[null,"You cannot measure harms before you have identified them, and mitigation must come before operating.","Mitigating before identifying and measuring means you do not know which harms to address or whether fixes work.","This is the reverse order; operating a system before identifying and mitigating harms is exactly what the process prevents."],null],["aif85",0,5,"A chat app built on an Azure OpenAI deployment must block hateful and violent content in both prompts and responses. What provides this by default?",["The model's temperature setting","Automated machine learning","The Azure pricing tier","Content filters in the safety system"],3,"Azure OpenAI deployments pass prompts and completions through content filters that detect categories such as hate, sexual, violence and self-harm at configurable severity levels. Temperature only affects randomness.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",["Temperature only changes randomness and does not block hateful or violent content.","Automated machine learning trains predictive models and has no role in filtering chat content.","The pricing tier affects cost and limits, not whether harmful content is blocked.",null],null],["aif86",0,5,"A social platform wants to check user-uploaded text and images for harmful content, separately from any chat app. Which service fits?",["Azure AI Language key phrase extraction","Azure AI Content Safety","Azure AI Document Intelligence","Azure Machine Learning designer"],1,"Azure AI Content Safety analyzes text and images for harmful content categories with severity levels, for any app. Key phrase extraction finds topics but does not assess harm.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",["Key phrase extraction lists topics but does not assess text or images for harmful content.",null,"Document Intelligence extracts fields from documents and does not moderate user uploads.","The designer builds ML pipelines and is not a content moderation service."],null],["aif87",0,5,"Which is an example of a mitigation at the system message and grounding layer for a generative AI app?",["Adding a banner that tells users responses are AI-generated","Choosing a different base model from the catalog","Instructing the model to answer only from provided documents","Setting up a monitoring dashboard after release"],2,"Instructions in the system message and grounding with trusted data are the metaprompt and grounding layer. Choosing a model is the model layer, a banner is the user experience layer, and monitoring belongs to operating the solution.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",["A banner disclosing AI-generated content is a user experience layer mitigation.","Choosing a different base model is a model layer mitigation.",null,"Monitoring after release belongs to the operate stage, not the system message and grounding layer."],null],["aif88",0,5,"An evaluation checks whether each answer from a RAG app is supported by the retrieved source documents rather than made up. Which metric is this?",["Groundedness","Fluency","Latency","Recall"],0,"Groundedness measures how well answers are supported by the provided context. Fluency judges how natural the language reads, which says nothing about whether it is supported by sources.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",[null,"Fluency judges how natural the language reads, not whether answers are supported by sources.","Latency measures response time and says nothing about whether answers are supported.","Recall is a classification metric about found positives, not whether generated text is backed by sources."],null],["aif89",0,5,"Before launch, a team deliberately tries to make its chatbot produce harmful or off-policy output so they can fix weaknesses. What is this practice called?",["Load testing","A/B testing","Red teaming","Fine-tuning"],2,"Red teaming means systematically probing an AI system for harmful outputs to identify risks before release. Load testing checks performance under traffic, not safety.","Responsible AI considerations for generative AI",["Load testing checks performance under heavy traffic, not whether the chatbot can be made to produce harmful output.","A/B testing compares two versions with real users to see which performs better, not probing for harmful behavior.",null,"Fine-tuning further trains a model; it is not a practice of trying to break it."],null],["aif90",0,5,"A chatbot confidently states a refund policy that does not exist. Which term describes this and what is a common mitigation?",["Overfitting; collect more training data for the base model","A hallucination; ground the model in real policy documents","Tokenization; shorten the user's prompt","Bias; switch the model to a higher temperature"],1,"Fabricated but fluent answers are often called hallucinations (ungrounded output). Grounding with RAG over the real policy and instructing the model to answer only from it reduces them; a higher temperature would make it worse.","Identify features of generative AI solutions",["Overfitting is a training issue with custom models; more base-model training data does not fix an invented policy.",null,"Tokenization is how text is split for a model; it does not describe a fabricated answer, and shortening prompts will not fix it.","Bias refers to unfair skew, and a higher temperature would make invented answers more likely, not less."],null]]);
